%erg 0.1
Title: erg-pr-merge: handle unavailable branch-rule inspection in direct fallback
Created: 2026-10-19
Author: Minh Ha-Duong

--- log ---
2026-10-19T06:01Z Minh Ha-Duong created

--- body ---
## Context

On 2026-10-19, `/merge` for climate-finance-het PR #1946 closed and pushed
the claimed ticket, then GitHub rejected auto-merge because the repository has
auto-merge disabled. The direct-merge fallback found an empty check rollup and
a mergeable PR, but aborted while inspecting `main` protection and effective
branch rules: both GitHub API calls returned HTTP 403 with a plan-gate message.
The author reports that this private repository has no classic branch
protection or rulesets and that the account is Pro. A normal `gh pr merge
--merge` succeeded afterwards.

`erg-pr-merge` therefore treated an unavailable inspection endpoint as a hard
stop even though a normal forge merge was possible. The API error is not
reliable evidence that rules exist or that required checks are configured. The
fallback should rely on the forge's ordinary merge enforcement when inspection
is denied, without bypassing rules or checks. Keep refusals for failing or
pending checks, and never use `--admin`.

## Relevant files

- `skills/merge/erg-pr-merge` — direct-merge fallback and branch-rule inspection
- `skills/merge/SKILL.md` — documented fallback behavior
- `tests/test_erg_pr_merge.sh` — stubbed forge regression coverage

## Actions

1. Define a safe fallback for permission/plan-gated branch-rule inspection.
2. Let the ordinary GitHub merge operation enforce any configured requirements;
   report its refusal clearly if a required check or rule blocks the merge.
3. Preserve the existing behavior for failing checks, pending checks, and
   unavailable or malformed PR status data. Do not use an administrator bypass.

## Test

Add a stubbed forge case where the rollup is empty, branch-protection/rules
inspection returns HTTP 403, and an ordinary merge succeeds. Add a negative
control showing that a forge refusal still fails without using an admin bypass.
Keep the existing failing-check and pending-check refusal cases green.

## Verification

- [ ] `tests/test_erg_pr_merge.sh` covers the denied-inspection success path and
      the ordinary merge refusal path.
- [ ] The ticket-only PR gate (`erg check` and the cross-PR ticket collision
      check) passes.

## Invariants

- No `--admin` or other bypass of the forge's configured merge requirements.
- Failing and pending status checks continue to prevent direct merge.
- Ticket close handling remains recoverable if a forge merge attempt fails.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] A GitHub plan/permission 403 during branch-rule inspection no longer
      prevents a normal, non-admin merge when the forge accepts it.
- [ ] If the forge rejects that merge, `/merge` reports the refusal and leaves
      the PR unmerged; no rules or checks are bypassed.
- [ ] The regression tests and ticket-only PR gate pass.

## Raid Imagine — 2026-10-19

Premise confirmed in `no_checks_required`: either inspection failure currently
aborts despite an explicitly empty rollup and mergeable PR. Preserve all exit
criteria. Prefer recognizing HTTP 403 only, delegating enforcement to ordinary
non-admin merge; other API errors and malformed successful data remain refusals.
No library dependency or new abstraction is needed. Estimated implementation:
under one author-hour, no new service spend.

Blind-spot pass: include simultaneous denial of both endpoints as reported,
alongside individual endpoint cases and forge refusal. No intent change.
